seg-ceee5ddc0c36 = On the WebAssembly face every entry point is a separate call: <c1/> (the result document as a JSON string), <c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/> (the scenario's CSV (comma-separated values) table as a string, or <c5/> for kinds that publish no table; it throws on an invalid scenario), <c6/> (one engine run returning <c7/> as a JSON string — use it when you want more than one output, since every other call runs the scenario afresh), <c8/> (the same outputs as one typed object, with the document already parsed as <c9/>), <c10/>, <c11/> / <c12/> / <c13/> (introspection; <c14/> returns the parsed list), <c15/> / <c16/> — the shareable-link (URL, uniform resource locator) codec <a1>Kshana Studio</a1> uses to round-trip a whole scenario through the address-bar fragment — and <c17/> / <c18/> / <c19/>, the SP3-c (revision c of the Standard Product { "3" } format) and CCSDS (Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems) ephemeris artifacts — OMM, the Orbit Mean-elements Message, and OEM, the Orbit Ephemeris Message — the CLI writes — and <c20/>, which assesses a real receiver log described by a <c21/> scenario and returns <c22/> as a JSON string (the browser has no file system, so the log, and any navigation file, must be inline in the scenario as <c23/> or <c24/>; a <c25/> table adds the maritime monitors and the 0-100 trust score, advisory only), <c26/> / <c27/> (which interoperability formats a scenario supports, without running it, and one export in memory: <c28/>, <c29/>, <c30/>, <c31/> or <c32/> as JSON <c33/>, byte-identical for the same scenario, nothing uploaded), <c34/> (a GeoJSON route written into a waypoint-track scenario, the command line's <c35/>), <c36/> (the run's time series as an animated SVG, a single HTML player or SVG frames), <c37/> / <c38/> (the bundled reference scenarios and their TOML), <c39/> (bind an RFC { "3161" } token to an evidence pack in memory; the pack must still verify, and the timestamp authority's signature is not checked), <c40/> (which satellites' Galileo navigation data an I/NAV page file or a UBX stream authenticates, with the trusted Merkle root or public keys and an optional independent reference time; it authenticates data and makes no anti-spoofing performance claim; nothing is uploaded), <c41/> (a bounded NMEA excerpt scored the way live mode scores it; no gate), <c42/> (a vessel's NMEA log as a batch run), <c43/> (a trust stream or result as Prometheus text, CEF or LEEF syslog lines or an OTLP payload; nothing is sent), <c44/> (check a signed evidence pack; nothing is uploaded; packs are made with the CLI, Python or MCP), <c45/> (a scenario's motion and events as text for a laboratory simulator; no signal is written), <c46/> and <c47/> (which public-framework requirement rows result documents support evidence for, with each row's gap; not a finding that a framework is met), and <c48/> (ADS-B or AIS CSV text to a <c49/> GeoJSON per UTC day; aggregate only), <c50/> (a route's share through degraded cells of those maps; not a forecast), <c51/> (synthetic bridge NMEA and an instructor log for crew training; text only, never for a vessel's live navigation systems) and the sky-geometry functions <c52/>, <c53/>, <c54/>, <c55/> and <c56/> (which satellites a site sees when buildings, quays or terrain block part of the sky, with the dilution of precision, the horizontal error ellipse, a seeded Monte Carlo cloud and a skyplot as SVG text; the visibility decision and the ellipse are checked against independent libraries, the presets are representative geometries rather than surveys, and a skyline removes line-of-sight satellites and nothing more). Not in the browser build: <c57/> with its gate and TCP listener, streamed training NMEA, and the telemetry exporters (Prometheus, OpenTelemetry, syslog) are long-running network processes and stay in the command-line tool; creating an evidence pack needs a signing key, which belongs with the CLI or Python. There is no one-call <c58/> here; that binding exists only on the Python wheel.

